        (97, 98): 256,
        (98, 99): 257,
        (256, 99): 258
    }

The value in `merges` acts as the merge rank/priority in our simple
implementation. A smaller rank means that the merge was learned earlier
during training and therefore has higher priority.

We start with:
    best_pair = None
    best_rank = infinity

Then we check every pair currently present in `stats`.

If the pair exists in `merges`, we get its rank. If its rank is smaller
than the best rank we have found so far, it becomes the new best pair.

After checking all pairs:
- If best_pair is still None, no learned merge can be applied, so we stop.
- Otherwise, we merge the best pair and repeat the process.

In short:
    stats  = which pairs currently exist
    merges = which pairs the tokenizer learned to merge
    rank   = priority of a learned merge
    smaller rank = higher priority
